  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n- Becoming wet, windy, and colder through Friday\n\nEverything remains on track for the evolution of our weather from \ntoday through Friday evening, with no major changes noted at this \ntime.\n\nPlenty of clouds remain from the stratocumulus clouds that moved \noverhead on Wednesday. There are some breaks in this, but high \nclouds are widespread, and the holes in the lower clouds are filling \nin. We will see deep moisture advect over the area this afternoon as \nthe strengthening low level jet associated ahead of the upper wave \nand developing sfc low will approach late this afternoon. It is with \nthis feature that we will see the most widespread rain with this \nentire system. The sliver of instability (elevated in nature based \nbetween 850 and 700 mb) that may affect the I-94 corridor would also \nbe maximized at that time. All of this would be through just before \n06z when the low level jet peels away and the occluded front moves \nthrough. \n\nThe next wave of showers will then come right ahead of the secondary \ncold front/cold conveyor belt that will surge in from the NW. This \ncomes through centered around 12z Friday give or take a couple of \nhours depending on the northern or southern portion of the forecast \narea. This is associated at the leading edge of the upper trough and \ncyclonic flow arriving. The passage of these features will \nultimately shift the mean low level flow to become from the NNE. \nLake effect rain showers will be likely initially from the NW, and \nwill shift offshore by late afternoon. It is from mid morning until \nthe wind shifts offshore when the best chance of lake induced \nthunder will be possible with just short of 1,000 J/kg of CAPE \navailable.\n\nWind will be another aspect of this system. Right now, it looks like \nmost places will see gusts to around 40 mph Friday. May just be \nshort of advisory criteria inland, where instability will be a bit \nless than over the warmer waters.\n\nA few showers will remain possible inland through the daylight \nhours, and a few may linger into mid-evening. It will be shortly \nafter 00z Saturday the the upper trough moves out, and the flow \nbecomes anti-cyclonic.  \n\n- Extended dry period with moderating temperatures next week\n\nStrong subsidence will build in quickly behind the departure of the \nupper trough by Saturday morning. The upper air pattern over the \nCONUS then transitions to a much more amplified trough/ridge/trough \npattern from the West Coast to East Coast in almost an Omega block. \n\nThe strong upper trough on Friday will close off along the East \nCoast, and strong energy moves toward the West Coast from the \nPacific. This will place Michigan in between under the influence of \nthe strong ridge. This means plenty of dry weather, with sunny days \nand cool nights. There will be attempts of short waves trying to \nflatten the ridge. It seems that the ridge should remain intact \nthrough at least mid-week. \n\nLater in the week, there have been indications that there may be a \ntrough that tries to close off overhead next Thursday. Way too much \nuncertainty seven days out to try to pin down the details. Generally \nspeaking, we will see above average temperatures, and below average \nprecipitation.",
